WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.496
Your payment service sends three authorizations to an issuer, and each takes the issuer 300 milliseconds.

2
00:00:09.496 --> 00:00:11.567
How soon are all three answered?

3
00:00:11.733 --> 00:00:15.844
HTTP/1.1 sends one request at a time on a connection.

4
00:00:15.844 --> 00:00:21.816
A response carries no label, so the next request waits for this one's answer.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.972
Three requests take 900 milliseconds.

6
00:00:24.972 --> 00:00:32.040
The usual fix is more connections, and each new one pays its own TCP and TLS handshakes.

7
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.129
HTTP/2 keeps one connection and labels everything.

8
00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:41.472
Each request and its response is a stream with a number: 1, 3 and 5.

9
00:00:41.633 --> 00:00:45.546
Messages are cut into frames, and each frame names its stream.

10
00:00:45.546 --> 00:00:49.775
Frames of all three take turns, and answers come back in any order.

11
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.786
Underneath, it's still TCP: one ordered stream of bytes.

12
00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:56.748
Say the three answers leave in three packets.

13
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:59.096
The packet with stream 1 is lost.

14
00:00:59.096 --> 00:01:02.045
The packets for streams 3 and 5 arrive whole.

15
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:05.711
But TCP hands nothing past a hole to your service.

16
00:01:05.711 --> 00:01:09.363
Streams 3 and 5 sit in the kernel, complete, waiting.

17
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:15.947
The resend takes at least a round trip, say 100 milliseconds, and all three requests wait for it.

18
00:01:15.947 --> 00:01:17.931
One loss stalled every stream.

19
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:24.903
At one percent loss, a busy connection hits several holes a second, and each one stalls everything in flight.

20
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.040
HTTP/3 moves the streams into the transport itself.

21
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.753
QUIC runs over UDP and keeps each stream in order on its own.

22
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:35.348
Lose the same packet.

23
00:01:35.348 --> 00:01:41.008
Streams 3 and 5 go straight to your service, and only stream 1 waits for its resend.

24
00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:47.293
HTTP/2 removed the queue inside HTTP and kept the one inside TCP.

25
00:01:47.293 --> 00:01:52.233
With HTTP/3, a lost packet costs only its own stream.
